WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:06.589
PostgreSQL FILTER gives us another way to calculate
conditional measures Instead

00:00:06.602 --> 00:00:10.220
of placing a CASE expression inside COUNT we write
the aggregate and

00:00:10.247 --> 00:00:15.749
then specify which rows are allowed to contribute to
that aggregate Run

00:00:15.776 --> 00:00:20.383
the example and compare its output with the previous
CASE version The

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:26.327
counts should express the same analytical idea
FILTER can be especially

00:00:26.353 --> 00:00:30.722
readable when a SELECT list contains several
independent conditional counts or sums

00:00:32.475 --> 00:00:36.422
If you are practicing in pgAdmin copy both versions
and compare them

00:00:36.448 --> 00:00:40.201
side by side The goal is not to decide that one is

00:00:40.227 --> 00:00:44.517
always better The goal is to recognize both patterns
and choose the

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:47.438
one that is clear and appropriate for the SQL system
you are

00:00:47.464 --> 00:00:47.872
using
